#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148789 :tutorial/content "We can find a percentage part of a number using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $16 \\%$ of $300$.\n\nHere, the whole is $300$ and the percentage is $16 \\%$. So, the part is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = 16\\% \\times 300\n$$\n\nBut how do we multiply a percentage by a number? We have two methods of doing this. Let's apply both and check that they give the same answer.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n16\\% = 16 \\div 100 = 0.16\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 16% \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= 0.16 \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16\\%$ of $300$ is $48$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n16\\% = \\dfrac{16}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 16% \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{100} \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{300}{100} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16 \\%$ of $300$ is $48$." :tutorial/id #uuid "41c3207b-f9a6-5e36-9a0b-a6ad0c9e8a18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8198 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
